  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist.\n\n**Data exploration:** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` view has 14,074 documents, all with `geo.src` = US. Its time field is `timestamp`. Response codes are only 200 (12,832), 404 (801), and 503 (441), so the only 5xx in this data is 503. The main fields are `response`, `bytes`, `machine.os`, `extension`, and `request`.\n\n**Dashboard:** `EFF-14713d5d Web logs overview` (id `eff-14713d5d-web-logs-overview-e341e5b6807d`) has five panels, with a 90-day time range:\n- Total requests (count)\n- Average response size (average of `bytes`)\n- Requests over time (area chart, date histogram on `timestamp`)\n- Requests by response code (pie, on `response.keyword`)\n- Top operating systems (bar, on `machine.os.keyword`)\n\n**Alert rule:** `EFF-14713d5d 5xx spike` (id `39783187-4f27-4d2c-8c61-3d0211ee08cc`) is an `.es-query` rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every 1m and fires when the count of documents matching `response.keyword` = `5??` is greater than 10 in a 5-minute window. I enabled it so it actually checks; its status is currently `pending` until the first run.\n\nChoices I made that you may want to change:\n- I matched 5xx with a `5??` wildcard, since `response` is text and a numeric range doesn't apply.\n- The rule has no connector attached, so it will track firings but won't send notifications.\n- I tagged the rule `EFF-14713d5d`.",
